INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A dye house''s shade-matching lab uses spectrophotometer-predicted dye recipes (software calculates the theoretical dye combination/percentages to match a target shade based on the dyes'' known absorption spectra) but STILL requires a physical lab dip trial before bulk approval, rather than trusting the software prediction directly. Why does the software''s spectral prediction alone remain insufficient for final approval?', '["Spectrophotometer prediction software is always 100% accurate with zero possible prediction error, making the physical lab dip trial step entirely unnecessary and purely a wasteful formality","The prediction software''s accuracy depends on having a complete, accurately-calibrated database of each dye''s absorption behavior at the ACTUAL process conditions (exhaustion%, fixation efficiency, specific fiber substrate) that will be used in the real dyeing — real-world factors like dye-to-dye interaction effects, the SPECIFIC fiber batch''s dye affinity, and process-condition variations from the software''s calibration data can all cause the actual physical result to deviate from the pure spectral prediction, so a physical trial remains necessary to verify the prediction actually holds under real conditions","Physical lab dip trials are performed purely as a legal/contractual formality with zero genuine technical verification purpose, since the software prediction is always trusted as definitively correct regardless of the physical trial''s result","Spectrophotometer software can only predict shades for a SINGLE dye, making it technically incapable of predicting any multi-dye combination recipe, which is why physical trials are needed for combination recipes specifically"]'::jsonb, 'The prediction software''s accuracy depends on having a complete, accurately-calibrated database of each dye''s absorption behavior at the ACTUAL process conditions (exhaustion%, fixation efficiency, specific fiber substrate) that will be used in the real dyeing — real-world factors like dye-to-dye interaction effects, the SPECIFIC fiber batch''s dye affinity, and process-condition variations from the software''s calibration data can all cause the actual physical result to deviate from the pure spectral prediction, so a physical trial remains necessary to verify the prediction actually holds under real conditions', 'This connects to the earlier lab-dip-to-bulk scale-up discussion — while spectrophotometer-based recipe prediction software is a genuinely powerful tool (dramatically narrowing down candidate recipes compared to pure trial-and-error), its predictions are only as accurate as its underlying calibration database, which can''t perfectly capture every real-world variable: dye-to-dye interaction effects when multiple dyes are combined, the SPECIFIC fiber batch''s actual dye affinity (which can vary from the calibration reference), and real process-condition variations — a physical lab dip trial remains the necessary final verification step to confirm the software''s theoretical prediction actually holds under real, physical dyeing conditions before committing to bulk production.', 3, '{"spectrophotometer-prediction-limitation"}'::text[]),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A finishing plant compares ''stenter'' (tenter frame) drying/heat-setting with pin/clip fabric edge-holding versus a ''relaxed dryer'' (fabric passed through drying chambers with minimal width-holding tension). For a KNIT fabric specifically (connecting to the earlier knit-shrinkage/compacting discussion), why might a mill deliberately choose the relaxed dryer approach DESPITE the stenter''s superior width/dimension CONTROL precision?', '["There is no valid reason to ever choose a relaxed dryer over a stenter for any fabric type; the stenter''s superior width control makes it unconditionally the better choice in every single finishing scenario","A stenter''s tension-holding action (pinning/clipping the fabric to a controlled width while drying/heat-setting) can lock in STRETCHED dimensions that don''t reflect the fabric''s natural RELAXED state — for a knit fabric specifically, this risks the exact opposite problem the earlier compacting discussion warned against: shipping fabric in an artificially stretched, tension-held state that will then shrink MORE than expected once the customer''s garment is washed and the fabric finally relaxes to its true dimensions — a relaxed dryer avoids this by allowing the fabric to dry/set closer to its natural, unstretched resting state","Relaxed dryers always produce WORSE dimensional stability than stenters for every fabric type including knits, making this described mill preference for relaxed drying technically irrational","Stenter and relaxed dryer technology produce mathematically identical fabric dimensional outcomes for knit fabric, making the choice between them purely about production speed with zero dimensional-stability consideration"]'::jsonb, 'A stenter''s tension-holding action (pinning/clipping the fabric to a controlled width while drying/heat-setting) can lock in STRETCHED dimensions that don''t reflect the fabric''s natural RELAXED state — for a knit fabric specifically, this risks the exact opposite problem the earlier compacting discussion warned against: shipping fabric in an artificially stretched, tension-held state that will then shrink MORE than expected once the customer''s garment is washed and the fabric finally relaxes to its true dimensions — a relaxed dryer avoids this by allowing the fabric to dry/set closer to its natural, unstretched resting state', 'This directly extends the earlier knit-shrinkage/compacting discussion with a specific finishing-equipment tradeoff — a stenter''s precise width-holding tension is valuable for WOVEN fabric (where dimensional precision matters and the fabric structure is less prone to this specific stretched-shipping-state problem), but for KNIT fabric, holding the fabric under tension while it dries/heat-sets can lock in an ARTIFICIALLY STRETCHED state that hasn''t been allowed to relax to its true resting dimensions — shipping in this stretched state means the fabric/garment will shrink MORE than expected once the customer''s first wash finally lets it relax naturally, exactly the residual-shrinkage risk the earlier compacting discussion specifically warned against — a relaxed dryer (minimal width-holding tension) avoids this by letting the knit fabric dry/set closer to its natural resting state, which is why relaxed drying (sometimes combined with compacting) is often preferred over stenter-style tension drying specifically for knit fabric.', 3, '{"stenter-vs-relaxed-dryer-knit"}'::text[]),
('9e3b3072-3153-414d-b2d7-1bf0789002ac', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A dye house''s technical bulletin distinguishes ''exhaustion%'' from ''fixation%'' as two SEPARATE efficiency metrics in reactive dyeing (connecting to the earlier reactive-dye-sequencing discussion). If a dye recipe shows 92% exhaustion but only 78% fixation, what does this GAP specifically indicate, and what happens to the UNFIXED portion of the exhausted dye?', '["Exhaustion% and fixation% always have to be numerically identical, making this described gap scenario technically impossible under standard reactive dyeing chemistry","The gap indicates that while 92% of the total dye successfully transferred from the bath onto/into the fiber (exhaustion), only 78% of the TOTAL dye actually formed a permanent covalent bond with the fiber (fixation) — the remaining 14 percentage points (92%−78%) represent dye that exhausted onto the fiber but then HYDROLYZED (reacted with water instead of the fiber) rather than fixing; this unfixed, hydrolyzed dye remains loosely associated with the fiber and must be REMOVED during the soaping-off/wash-off step (as established earlier), or it will cause poor wet/wash fastness in the finished product","The 14-percentage-point gap between exhaustion and fixation represents dye that was never added to the bath at all, unrelated to any hydrolysis or wash-off consideration","A fixation% LOWER than exhaustion% is impossible under any real dyeing chemistry, making this specific 92%/78% data combination technically implausible and requiring no further interpretation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap indicates that while 92% of the total dye successfully transferred from the bath onto/into the fiber (exhaustion), only 78% of the TOTAL dye actually formed a permanent covalent bond with the fiber (fixation) — the remaining 14 percentage points (92%−78%) represent dye that exhausted onto the fiber but then HYDROLYZED (reacted with water instead of the fiber) rather than fixing; this unfixed, hydrolyzed dye remains loosely associated with the fiber and must be REMOVED during the soaping-off/wash-off step (as established earlier), or it will cause poor wet/wash fastness in the finished product', 'This directly extends the earlier reactive-dye exhaustion-vs-fixation and fixation-failure/fastness discussions with a precise numeric interpretation — exhaustion% measures how much dye left the bath and associated with the fiber, while fixation% measures how much of the TOTAL dye actually formed the permanent covalent bond; the gap between them (here, 92%−78%=14 percentage points) represents dye that exhausted onto the fiber but then hydrolyzed (reacted with water rather than fiber, as established in the earlier premature-alkali discussion) instead of fixing — this hydrolyzed dye remains only loosely, physically associated with the fiber and MUST be thoroughly removed during soaping-off, or it directly causes the poor wet/wash fastness and bleeding problems discussed earlier, making the exhaustion-fixation GAP itself a genuinely diagnostic quality indicator, not just two independently interesting numbers.', 3, '{"exhaustion-fixation-gap-interpretation"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
